      body: 'Wanjiru Kamau was a nurse at a hospital in Nairobi, Kenya. The year her husband died she was left with two sons, a rent she could barely make, and night shifts that ran into the morning. She did not feel strong. But every morning before the bus, she sat her boys at the small table, read one verse aloud, and prayed. Some mornings she prayed through tears. Some mornings the only prayer she had was, Lord, help me do what You say today. She did it anyway, the same table, the same hour, all year. The next rainy season a bus overturned on the Mombasa road, and the ward filled in an hour. A young nurse named Achieng froze in the hallway, shaking. Wanjiru took her by the arm and walked her through it, one patient at a time, calm the whole night. At dawn Achieng asked her how she stayed so steady. Wanjiru said, I did not get strong in one day. He built me one morning at a time. Then she wrote two verses on the back of a chart for her. "And not only so, but we glory in tribulations also: knowing that tribulation worketh patience;" (Romans 5:3) "But the God of all grace, who hath called us unto his eternal glory by Christ Jesus, after that ye have suffered a while, make you perfect, stablish, strengthen, settle you." (1 Peter 5:10)',
